Until now, getting real-time speed out of an LLM meant giving something up. You dropped to a smaller model, accepted a narrower task, or told the user to wait. OpenAI's [Ultrafast preview](https://openai.com/index/previewing-ultrafast/), announced August 13, 2026, is an attempt to break that trade: GPT-5.6 Sol, the flagship, running up to 14 times faster than Standard processing.

This post covers what GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast actually is, how 750 output tokens per second compares to the tiers you already use, which workloads it unlocks, what it costs, and the thing most teams will get wrong about it. If you want the model family background first, see our [GPT-5.6 launch breakdown](/blog/post/gpt-56-is-here-openais-efficient-frontier-model).

# What is GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast?

**GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast is a new OpenAI service tier that runs GPT-5.6 Sol at up to 14 times the speed of Standard processing, generating up to 750 output tokens per second. It runs on Cerebras hardware, launches first in the OpenAI API, and is in limited preview with a select group of customers as of August 13, 2026.**

The key detail is what does not change: Ultrafast is a service tier, not a new model. It runs GPT-5.6 Sol, with the difference being how quickly the model generates responses.

The key details are:

* **Type:** Service tier, not a new model.
* **Speed:** Up to 14x faster than Standard processing.
* **Throughput:** Up to 750 output tokens per second.
* **Hardware:** Runs on Cerebras hardware.
* **Availability:** Currently in limited preview through the OpenAI API.
* **Intelligence:** The same GPT-5.6 Sol model, with the difference being how quickly it generates responses.

# How fast is 750 tokens per second compared to Fast mode?

OpenAI now has three processing speeds for Sol, and the gaps between them are large enough to change what you can build.

Standard is the baseline. [Fast mode](/blog/post/openai-cuts-gpt-56-pricing-and-adds-fast-mode-to-the-api), which replaced Priority Processing in July 2026, runs about 2.5 times faster at twice the price. Ultrafast sits far above both.

| Tier      | Speed vs Standard | Approximate output tokens/sec | Status              |
| --------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------- |
| Standard  | 1x                | \~54                          | Generally available |
| Fast mode | Up to 2.5x        | \~134                         | Generally available |
| Ultrafast | Up to 14x         | Up to 750                     | Limited preview     |

The Standard and Fast mode token rates are implied by OpenAI's own 14x and 750 tokens per second figures rather than published directly, so treat them as directional. The ratio is the part that matters: Ultrafast is roughly 5.6 times faster than Fast mode, which was already the premium option.

Translate that into wall-clock time. A 2,000-token answer that takes about 37 seconds on Standard lands in under 3 seconds on Ultrafast. That is the difference between a progress spinner and a conversation.

OpenAI's own demo makes the point without numbers: Ultrafast and Standard building the same working 3D warehouse simulator from the same text prompt, side by side, with one finishing while the other is still generating.

# Why GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast runs on Cerebras hardware

Ultrafast is the next step in OpenAI's partnership with [Cerebras](https://www.cerebras.ai/), which builds wafer-scale accelerators designed around inference latency rather than raw training throughput.

The reason this matters is architectural. Ultrafast marks the next step in OpenAI's partnership with Cerebras to bring ultra-low-latency inference to its platform. OpenAI says Cerebras is supporting GPT-5.6 Sol at up to 750 output tokens per second, allowing businesses to build more responsive products and workflows.

The practical read: this is not a distilled or quantized Sol running on the same stack. It is the same model on hardware built for a different bottleneck. That is why OpenAI can claim a 14x speedup with no intelligence trade-off, which is a claim you should normally be skeptical of.

# Which workloads GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast unlocks

When frontier intelligence stops costing you 30 seconds, it moves into parts of a business that previously could not wait for it. OpenAI names five categories from its preview customers.

* **Incident response and reliability:** Analyze logs, recent commits, and engineer reports to identify a likely cause while the outage is still unfolding.
* **Financial research and security:** Assess transactions and flag suspicious activity while market conditions are still changing.
* **Customer support and voice:** Resolve multi-step issues in real time without a dead-air pause in the conversation.
* **Commerce:** Answer product questions, check inventory, and fix checkout problems while the shopper is still deciding.
* **Live research and experimentation:** Turn an overnight experiment run into an interactive working session.

The common thread is not that these tasks are hard. It is that they all have a deadline set by something outside your system: an outage clock, a market tick, a person breathing on a phone call, a shopper's patience. Below a certain latency the model participates. Above it, the moment has passed.

Voice is the sharpest example. Conversational turn-taking breaks down past roughly a second of silence, which is why voice agents have historically been built on small models. Ultrafast makes it more practical to use frontier intelligence in real-time voice experiences, even when finding the answer requires multiple steps or systems.

Early preview customers span coding, commerce, financial research, and support, including Jane Street, [Podium](https://www.podium.com/), Basis, and [Rogo](https://rogo.ai/).

# How OpenAI uses Ultrafast internally


**Incident response.** When an alert fires, engineers use Ultrafast to read logs, analyze traces, synthesize conversations, identify the next checks, and help prepare or validate a fix. The value is not that the model does something new. It is that the delay between observing a signal, testing a hypothesis, and choosing the next action collapses, while the evidence is still changing. Engineers keep responsibility for judgment and deployment.

**Research.** The standard research loop is to launch a batch of experiments overnight and review results in the morning, which caps you at one iteration per day. OpenAI reports that loop tightening to multiple iterations within a single workday. Changing the iteration count from one to several per day is a larger effect than most model upgrades produce.

Both examples describe the same shift. Speed is not making individual answers better. It is increasing how many times you get to ask.

# What does GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast cost?

**OpenAI has not published Ultrafast pricing. It is in limited preview with a select group of customers, and access expands as Cerebras capacity grows.** For now, the only way in is the waitlist on OpenAI's announcement.

You can still budget for it using the tiers that do have public numbers. GPT-5.6 Sol is $5 per million input tokens and $30 per million output tokens on Standard, and Fast mode doubles that in exchange for 2.5x speed. Both OpenAI's Fast mode and [Anthropic's fast mode on Claude Opus 4.8](/blog/post/anthropic-just-launched-claude-opus-48-with-fast-mode-and-dynamic-workflows) settled on a 2x multiplier for 2.5x speed. Current rates are on OpenAI's [API pricing page](https://platform.openai.com/docs/pricing).

Ultrafast is a much bigger jump on scarcer hardware, so pricing it against those precedents would be a guess. Plan for a premium tier you apply selectively, the same way Fast mode is meant to be used: pay for speed only on requests where a person or a deadline is waiting, and leave batch jobs, evaluation runs, and background processing on Standard.

# Should you build on GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast?

Ultrafast is worth pursuing when latency is genuinely your constraint. It is worth ignoring when it is not, and plenty of teams cannot tell the difference.

Reach for Ultrafast when:

* A human is waiting in real time, especially in voice or live chat.
* The task has an external deadline, such as an active incident or a moving market.
* Your workflow is iteration-bound, where more attempts per hour beats a better single attempt.
* The task genuinely needs Sol-level reasoning and a smaller model has already failed your evaluations.

Stay on Standard when:

* The work runs in the background and nobody is watching the clock.
* [GPT-5.6 Luna or Terra](/blog/post/openai-cuts-gpt-56-pricing-and-adds-fast-mode-to-the-api) already meets your quality bar, since Luna at $0.20 per million input tokens is both fast and cheap.
* Your p95 latency is dominated by tool calls, retrieval, or database round trips rather than token generation.

That last one is where most teams misdiagnose the problem. Measure before you chase a faster tier: instrument time-to-first-token, tokens-per-second, and total request time separately. If generation is 40% of your p95 and network plus tool calls are the rest, a 14x faster model gives you nowhere near a 14x faster product.

At 750 tokens per second, the model may stop being the slowest part of the request. Your infrastructure can become the bottleneck instead. A cold-start function, an unindexed query, or a chatty auth check that was previously hidden behind 30 seconds of generation can suddenly become the thing your user is waiting on.

# Build real-time GPT-5.6 apps on Appwrite

If Ultrafast removes model latency from your critical path, whatever is left has to keep up. A real-time AI feature still needs to authenticate the user, look up context, call OpenAI with a key that never touches the client, stream tokens to the browser, and persist the result. Each of those is a round trip, and hand-wiring them is where a sub-second budget quietly disappears.

[Appwrite](/) is an open source backend as a service that covers those pieces in one project:

* [Functions](/docs/products/functions) to call the OpenAI API server-side and keep your key off the client.
* [Databases](/docs/products/databases) for conversation history and retrieved context, with [indexed queries](/docs/products/databases/queries) so lookups do not become your new bottleneck.
* [Realtime](/docs/apis/realtime) to push tokens and state changes to connected clients as they arrive instead of polling.
* [Auth](/docs/products/auth) for accounts and sessions, and [Storage](/docs/products/storage) for uploads that feed into prompts.
* [Sites](/docs/products/sites) to deploy your frontend next to the backend it calls, served over Appwrite's [global network](/docs/products/network).

Run it on [managed Cloud](https://cloud.appwrite.io/) or [self-host](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) it. If you want the longer version of what a real-time AI feature actually requires underneath, our guide to [what an AI backend needs](/blog/post/what-is-an-ai-backend) walks through it.

Building with Codex? The [Appwrite plugin for Codex](/blog/post/announcing-appwrite-codex-plugin) ships agent skills for the Appwrite CLI and major SDKs and registers the Appwrite Docs MCP server, so your GPT-5.6 agent writes real SDK calls against a backend that already exists.

`codex plugin marketplace add appwrite/codex-plugin`

[Create a free Appwrite project](https://cloud.appwrite.io/), add the plugin, and get the rest of your stack fast enough to deserve a 14x model.

